---
.title = "Rooms across directories",
.description = "Hold rooms on any number of directories in one session, and message anyone you meet in them. The directories never talk to each other.",
.date = .date("2026-10-12T00:00:00"),
.layout = "page.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

A directory is a Rooms server. It lists rooms and hands out nicknames. You
are not limited to the one that served your page: one session can hold rooms
on as many directories as you want, and you can message anyone you meet in
any of them.

If you have used an IRC client connected to several networks, this will
feel familiar. The directories do not know about each other, and your
session connects to each one on its own. Unlike IRC, your direct messages do
not go through any directory. You can share your whole session with limits you
set, and a directory can point people at a room on another server without
trusting that server.

[One session, three directories. The sidebar holds a room on the directory that served the page and a room on each of two others, each named by the directory it is on, and a direct conversation with a member of each. The open conversation is with lu, who lives on the third directory.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-directories-dark.webp'))

[One session, three directories. The sidebar holds a room on the directory that served the page and a room on each of two others, each named by the directory it is on, and a direct conversation with a member of each. The open conversation is with lu, who lives on the third directory.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-directories-light.webp'))

In the picture, the page came from the directory that hosts `workshop`.
From there, mira joined `zine-swap` and `night-sky` on two other
directories by pasting their addresses into the join card. Then someone in
each room pressed the message button next to her name. None of the three
directories has heard of the other two, and none of them can read the
direct messages.

# Join a room on another directory

1. In the sidebar, press **+** beside **Your rooms** and type the room's name.
   The line under it says which directory the room will be on.
2. Press **Change** there. The join card comes up with the name filled in.
3. Paste the other directory's address into its address field and join.

A directory's address is at `/locator` on its page, and Settings copies it for
any directory you already hold.

The rooms you already had stay put. Once you hold more than one directory,
each row in the sidebar says which one it is on, and the one that served your
page shows as *this directory*. With a single directory there is nothing to
tell apart, so the sidebar leaves it out.

# What each directory owns

Each directory gives you your own nickname there. You can be `mira` on one
and `m` on another, and two directories can each have an `alice` who are
different people. A direct conversation belongs to the directory that
introduced you, since the person on the other end is a nickname that
directory gave out.

Leaving a directory takes its rooms, its direct conversations, and your
nickname there with it. Your other directories are not affected. Rooms asks
before you leave and lists what will go, and it never leaves a directory on
its own just because you left the last room there.

# Your session holds all of it

No directory knows where else you are. Each one knows your nickname there,
the rooms you joined there, and who you talk to there. Only your session
knows all of it.

Everything travels with your session. Share it with your phone and
every room on every directory is there, along with the same direct
conversations. Reply to someone from the phone and it reaches them on their
own directory, even though the phone never connected to it. Run your
session on a server with the session service and it stays in all of those
places while your laptop is closed.

[The same session opened on a phone. The phone only loaded the page from the first directory, but all three rooms and all three direct conversations came with the session, each still marked with its directory.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-directories-phone-dark.webp'))

[The same session opened on a phone. The phone only loaded the page from the first directory, but all three rooms and all three direct conversations came with the session, each still marked with its directory.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-directories-phone-light.webp'))

Sharing does not have to mean handing over everything. A session link
carries only the permissions you give it. You can make a link that lets
someone watch every room and conversation, on every directory, without
being able to post, and decide separately whether it can read the earlier
history. You can pause that link or take it back while they still hold it,
and nothing changes on the directories. Each one keeps seeing the same
session it always saw.

Use this to give a bot or a teammate a read-only view of everything you are
in. [Run Rooms]($link.page('rooms/start'))
shares a session with a phone, and
[Operate Rooms]($link.page('rooms/operations')) covers setting a link's
permissions.

# Direct messages go between the two of you

Chat that spans servers usually carries your messages through them.
[Matrix](https://matrix.org/) copies each room, encrypted or not, to every
server with a member in it. An IRC bouncer keeps you on several networks at
once, but each network still carries everything you say there, private
messages included.

In Rooms, the directory you share with someone introduces the two of you,
and from then on your messages go straight between you. If your network
will not allow that, you can choose a relay instead, and it only ever sees
encrypted bytes.

A directory carries its own rooms and nothing else. Nothing is copied
between directories, and no directory has any say over another's rooms. If a directory goes away, its
rooms go with it and everything else keeps working.

The cost is that names are local. A nickname only means something on the
directory that gave it out, so `lu` on one directory is not known to be the
same person as `lu` on another.

# A directory can list a room hosted elsewhere

A host can also put another directory's room in its own list. The host
opens the **Peering** section of their admin page, uses their peering link
to join the other room, and the room shows up in their directory's list. A member who picks
it from that list connects straight to the server that hosts the room. The
directory that listed it only made the introduction.

Whoever published a listing holds the one way to take it down, and the
listing also ends when their session disconnects. Either way, new members
stop finding the room there, and anyone who already joined keeps it.
[Operate Rooms]($link.page('rooms/operations')) covers the peering link.

# How OCapN makes this work

In [OCapN](https://ocapn.org/), a room, your seat in it, and your inbox for
direct messages are all references, and a reference works the same no
matter which server it came from. Joining a room on another directory is
fetching a reference from a different server. Starting a direct
message is OCapN's three-party handoff: the directory you share introduces
two peers, and they connect to each other. Peering a room is that same
handoff between a member and the room's host. Rooms has no federation
protocol on top of these.

[How Rooms works]($link.page('rooms/architecture')) follows a direct message
from introduction to connection, and
[The Rooms protocol]($link.page('rooms/protocol')) lists every reference a
directory hands out.
